description:
Move `tools/wiki-audio/worker/` (currently inside the wiki content repo)
into `worker/` of the new dedicated wiki-audio repo. Then delete the
directory from the wiki repo so there is exactly one source of truth.

## Why we keep the worker in the tool repo (not the wiki repo)

The worker is part of the tool's release surface — its TypeScript source
should ship and version with the Go binary, not with the prose. Otherwise
a worker change would be invisible from the wiki-audio releases history.

## Steps

1. After Phase B creates the new repo, `git mv tools/wiki-audio/worker
   <new-repo>/worker` (preserving history if practical, otherwise plain
   copy is acceptable — there is no real git history here yet).
2. `cd <new-repo>/worker && wrangler deploy` to confirm the deploy still
   works from the new location.
3. From the wiki repo: `git rm -r tools/wiki-audio/worker` and commit
   with a message that points at the new repo.

## Risks

- Worker secret `ACCESS_TOKEN` is per-Cloudflare-account, not per-repo,
  so the running Worker keeps its secret across the move. No re-secret
  step needed.
- Be sure wrangler.jsonc names the same Worker (`wiki-audio`) so deploy
  updates the existing Worker rather than creating a new one and leaving
  a stale Worker live.

## Worker source code (canonical — §9.2)

The Worker source MUST exactly match this skeleton (any deviation
should pass the wa-3ia.5 contract test). Embedded here so the source
is reconstructable from the beads alone if the repo is ever lost.

`worker/src/index.ts` — ~30 lines:

```typescript
export interface Env {
  BUCKET: R2Bucket;
  ACCESS_TOKEN: string;
}

function timingSafeEqual(a: string, b: string): boolean {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
  let out = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) out |= a.charCodeAt(i) ^ b.charCodeAt(i);
  return out === 0;
}

export default {
  async fetch(req: Request, env: Env): Promise<Response> {
    const url = new URL(req.url);
    const token = url.searchParams.get("t") ?? "";
    if (!timingSafeEqual(token, env.ACCESS_TOKEN)) {
      return new Response("forbidden", { status: 403 });
    }
    const key = url.pathname.replace(/^\/+/, "");
    if (!key) return new Response("not found", { status: 404 });

    const obj = await env.BUCKET.get(key);
    if (!obj) return new Response("not found", { status: 404 });

    const headers = new Headers();
    obj.writeHttpMetadata(headers);
    headers.set("etag", obj.httpEtag);
    headers.set("cache-control", "private, max-age=3600");
    return new Response(obj.body, { headers });
  },
};
```

## Load-bearing properties of this code

1. **OR-fold XOR loop** — defeats timing oracles. Naive `===` would
   short-circuit at the first mismatched character and leak token
   bytes via response-time measurement. The loop runs to completion
   for every input.
2. **Length check first** — short-circuiting on length is fine; the
   token length (43) is public and constant.
3. **Token check BEFORE key parsing** — never let an attacker
   distinguish "wrong token" from "wrong token AND wrong key". The
   3-status contract (403/403/404) is wa-3ia.2's verification.
4. **`cache-control: private`** — prevents Cloudflare's edge from
   caching responses across token values. Without this, a leaked
   token's cached objects could outlive rotation.
5. **R2 binding** (not signed URL) — the bucket has zero public
   access enabled. The Worker is the only path to reading objects.

## What MUST NOT change without a §9 re-read

- The OR-fold pattern — replacing with `===` is a regression.
- The order of checks (token before key).
- The cache-control header (must remain `private`).

`worker/wrangler.jsonc` MUST contain:
- `name: "wiki-audio"` (deploys to existing Worker, doesn't fork).
- R2 binding `BUCKET` → bucket `wiki-audio`.
- A secret declaration for `ACCESS_TOKEN` (set via `wrangler secret put`).
- NO `workers_dev: true` flag (no public dev URL surface; only the
  configured route).

